    "stratum": "wago",
    "processes": ["sound-change"],
    "headline": "Shikashi is possibly a shift from 然り (shikari, “being like so”) plus し.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "/ɕikari ɕi/ → /ɕikaɕːi/ → /ɕikaɕi/"
      },
      {
        "quote": "Possibly a shift from 然(しか)り (shikari, “being like so”, the 連(れん)用(よう)形(けい) (ren'yōkei, “stem or continuative form”) of classical verb 然(しか)り (shikari, “to be like so”)) + し (shi, the 連体形 (rentaikei, “attributive form”) of past recollective auxiliary き (ki)), contracting from shikari shi to shikasshi, and then further to shikashi."
      },
      {
        "quote": "The initial sense may have been parseable as that [preceding statement] being so, ..., much like English phrases such as be that as it may or that said, followed by a statement in opposition to the earlier statement."
      }
    ],
    "wiktionaryDump": "2026-09-02"
  },
